UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 4: Food safety and hygiene', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor. Detergent lifts grease and food. Rinsing removes the detergent. Air drying or a clean dry towel finishes the job so microbes are not wiped back on. Keep a separate cloth for the floor. A bench cloth that falls on the floor goes into the wash.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA sponge smells sour and is used for dishes and the floor. What is the problem, and what should replace that habit?\n\nFEEDBACK\nOne sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes. Use a washed dish cloth for dishes only, wash or replace it often, and use a different cloth for the floor.\n\nWATCH FOR\nRinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite the wash–rinse–dry steps for a chopping board and a measuring cup. Say what you would do if the only towel is already wet and dirty.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor. Detergent lifts grease and food. Rinsing removes the detergent. Air drying or a clean dry towel finishes the job so microbes are not wiped back on. Keep a separate cloth for the floor. A bench cloth that falls on the floor goes into the wash.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-SAF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write the wash–rinse–dry steps for a chopping board and a measuring cup. Say what you would do if the only towel is already wet and dirty. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using equipment on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible.. Correction: One sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating equipment on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with equipment, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about equipment with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for equipment. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for equipment. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["One sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work"],"question":"A sponge smells sour and is used for dishes and the floor. What is the problem, and what should replace that habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for equipment; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor","One sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes","Careful use of detergent with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named detergent, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","Name detergent only, without linking it to equipment","Apply equipment to new material and name the detergent used"],"question":"Which response best applies equipment with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on equipment. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","One sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves equipment","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Dirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name detergent only, with no linked example","Write the wash–rinse–dry steps for a chopping board and a measuring cup","Retype the worked example with no new example","Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible"],"question":"Which independent response best applies equipment to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about equipment with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating equipment on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using equipment on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for equipment. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with equipment, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible.. Correction: One sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) says Stage 4 teachers may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). This lesson uses FT5-SAF-01 as a pending proposal. The 2019 syllabus remains in teach-out. Not NESA verified. Practical cooking is optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise equipment.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use equipment with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify detergent and explain why it matters.","I can practise drying independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cloth needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor.","options":["Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wiping the knife on a tea towel that already wiped the bench is not the same control.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA sponge smells sour and is used for dishes and the floor. What is the problem, and what should replace that habit?\n\nFEEDBACK\nOne sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes. Use a washed dish cloth for dishes only, wash or replace it often, and use a different cloth for the floor.","explain":"Clean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor. Detergent lifts grease and food. Rinsing removes the detergent. Air drying or a clean dry towel finishes the job so microbes are not wiped back on. Keep a separate cloth for the floor. A bench cloth that falls on the floor goes into the wash.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A floor cloth must not wipe plates; wash, rinse and dry instead.","caption":"Separate cloths for floor and dishes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Not clean</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">floor cloth on plate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Clean</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wash, rinse, dry</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Dirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wi Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Dirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wi Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wiping the knife on a tea towel that already wiped the bench is not the same control. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Wash the knife before it touches apple.","caption":"A wipe on a used towel is not a wash.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Knife</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">peanut butter left</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Next use</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">washed, then apple</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible. Here is a more productive model: Dirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wiping the knife on a tea towel that already wiped the bench is not the same control.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nOne sour sponge spreads dirt from the floor onto dishes. Use a washed dish cloth for dishes only, wash or replace it often, and use a different cloth for the floor.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Rinsing a plate under cold water with no detergent and calling it clean because no crumbs are visible. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equipment?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nClean equipment means washed, rinsed and dried, not just wiped with a damp cloth that has already wiped the floor. Detergent lifts grease and food. Rinsing removes the detergent. Air drying or a clean dry towel finishes the job so microbes are not wiped back on. Keep a separate cloth for the floor. A bench cloth that falls on the floor goes into the wash. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 4: Food safety and hygiene","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Food safety and hygiene","week":3,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nDirty knife with peanut butter: wash in hot soapy water, rinse, dry with a clean towel, then use it for apple slices. Wiping the knife on a tea towel that already wiped the bench is not the same control.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A floor cloth must not wipe plates; wash, rinse and dry instead.","caption":"Separate cloths for floor and dishes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Not clean</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">floor cloth on plate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Clean</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wash, rinse, dry</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Wash the knife before it touches apple.","ca